# 2 — C, forces.  A rod hinged to a wall, held by a cable (3-4-5 geometry).
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 2, "id": "S05-02", "module": "C", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "A hinged rod held by a cable: the tension from moments",
    "rel": [("C", "Taking moments about the hinge removes the unknown hinge force"),
            ("A", "Resolving the cable tension into vertical and horizontal parts"),
            ("A", "The 3-4-5 triangle gives the cable's direction without trigonometry")],
    "key": ["moments", "cable", "triangle345"],
    "stem": '<p>A uniform-free horizontal rod <code>AB</code> is 4.0 m long and is hinged to a wall at <code>A</code>. A cable joins <code>B</code> to a point <code>C</code> that is 3.0 m vertically above <code>A</code>; the cable is therefore 5.0 m long. A weight of 150 N acts at the middle of the rod and a load of 45 N hangs from <code>B</code>. {{FIG:s05-02}}</p><p>What is the tension in the cable?</p>',
    "opts": ['150 N', '250 N', '200 N', '120 N', '160 N'],
    "ans": 2,
    "distractors": ['the 150 N load alone, ignoring both the 45 N at the end and the cable geometry', 'uses the full 5.0 m cable length as the moment arm of the tension, not its 3.0 m rise', 'correct', 'divides the total moment by the rod length without the 3/5 vertical factor of the cable', 'divides by the horizontal arm but forgets to multiply the vertical component by 4.0 m'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "the cable, rod and wall form a 3-4-5 right triangle, so the cable makes a 3/5 vertical, 4/5 horizontal triangle with the horizontal"),
            ("relate", "taking moments about the hinge A removes the two unknown hinge forces in one step"),
            ("eliminate", "the vertical component of the tension is T times 3/5, and it acts at B, 4.0 m from A"),
            ("solve", "equate clockwise and anticlockwise moments: (150 x 2.0) + (45 x 4.0) = (3T/5) x 4.0"),
            ("check", "the answer must be larger than either load, because the cable pulls at an angle and only part of it balances the moments"),
        ],
        "relations": ["T_y = T x 3/5", "150 x 2.0 + 45 x 4.0 = (3T/5) x 4.0", "T = 200 N"],
        "insight": "The 3-4-5 triangle is the whole question. Once you see that only the vertical component of the cable does any turning about the hinge, the moments equation is a single line, and the 5.0 m length is a red herring that never enters it.",
        "shape": "diagram-geometry",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "((150*2 + 45*4)*5)/(4*3)", "want": "200"},
    "sol": '<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you can take moments about a hinge to dispose of the unknown reaction, and whether you resolve the cable correctly -- only its vertical part turns the rod about <code>A</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Step 1 — the cable\'s direction.</b> The rod is 4.0 m out, the cable rises 3.0 m to the wall, so the cable, the rod and the wall make a 3-4-5 triangle. The tension therefore has a vertical component <code>T_y = T x 3/5</code> and a horizontal component <code>T x 4/5</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2 — choose the pivot.</b> About the hinge <code>A</code> the two hinge reactions have zero moment arm, so they vanish from the equation. The only unknown left is <code>T</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Step 3 — the clockwise moments.</b> The 150 N load sits at the midpoint, 2.0 m from <code>A</code>: moment <code>150 x 2.0 = 300 N m</code>. The 45 N load hangs at <code>B</code>, 4.0 m from <code>A</code>: moment <code>45 x 4.0 = 180 N m</code>. Total clockwise = <code>480 N m</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Step 4 — the anticlockwise moment.</b> Only the vertical component of the cable turns the rod, and it acts at <code>B</code> (4.0 m from <code>A</code>):</p>\n<div class="formula">T_y x 4.0 = (3T/5) x 4.0 = 12T/5</div>\n<p><b>Step 5 — solve.</b></p>\n<div class="formula">12T/5 = 480\nT = 480 x 5 / 12 = 200 N</div>\n<p>So <b>Answer: C.</b></p>\n<p><b>Sanity check.</b> The cable pulls at an angle, so its full tension must exceed the 150 N and 45 N loads that it balances between them; 200 N is just above that, which is the right direction. The 5.0 m cable length never appears, because only the 3.0 m rise does any vertical work.</p>\n<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>\n<p>&middot; <b>150 N</b> is the mid-rod load on its own -- a candidate who balances only that and forgets the end load and the cable angle lands here.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>250 N</b> uses the full 5.0 m cable as the tension\'s moment arm, treating the cable as if it pulled straight down.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>120 N</b> divides the total moment by the 4.0 m rod but omits the 3/5 vertical factor, so it under-values the cable.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>160 N</b> divides by the horizontal arm but forgets to multiply the vertical component by the 4.0 m distance from the hinge.</p>\n<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> moments about a pivot; resolving a tension; the 3-4-5 triangle; why the hypotenuse is a distraction.</p>',
    "trap": "Using the 5.0 m cable length as the moment arm of the tension. Only the 3.0 m vertical rise (the 3/5 component) does any turning about the hinge; the hypotenuse never enters the equation.",
},

