# 3 — H, circuits.  Two dividers across one supply, a voltmeter bridging the midpoints.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 3, "id": "S05-03", "module": "H", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "Two potential dividers: the voltmeter reading between their midpoints",
    "rel": [("H", "A potential divider splits the supply in proportion to the resistances"),
            ("H", "An ideal voltmeter draws no current and reads the potential difference between two points"),
            ("A", "Working branch currents first, then the drop across each resistor")],
    "key": ["divider", "voltmeter", "pdsplit"],
    "stem": '<p>A 12 V supply feeds two branches in parallel. The left branch is 2.0 ohm above its midpoint X and 6.0 ohm below it; the right branch is 6.0 ohm above its midpoint Y and 3.0 ohm below it. An ideal voltmeter bridges X and Y. {{FIG:s05-03}}</p><p>What does the voltmeter read?</p>',
    "opts": ['3.0 V', '8.0 V', '9.0 V', '1.5 V', '5.0 V'],
    "ans": 4,
    "distractors": ['the drop across the 2.0 ohm resistor alone, not the potential of X itself', 'the drop across the 6.0 ohm resistor on the right branch, a different quantity', 'the potential of point X measured from the bottom rail, not the difference X minus Y', 'the branch current written as if it were a voltage', 'correct'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "each branch is a divider; its current is 12 V divided by the total resistance of that branch"),
            ("relate", "the potential of a midpoint is found from the drop across the resistor below it (or above it)"),
            ("eliminate", "point X sits 9.0 V above the bottom rail and point Y sits 4.0 V above it, so the meter reads the difference"),
            ("solve", "V_X = 12 x 6/(2 + 6) = 9.0 V and V_Y = 12 x 3/(6 + 3) = 4.0 V, giving 9.0 - 4.0 = 5.0 V"),
            ("check", "the reading is positive and smaller than the supply, as a difference of two internal potentials should be"),
        ],
        "relations": ["I_left = 12/(2+6)", "V_X = I_left x 6", "I_right = 12/(6+3)", "V_Y = I_right x 3", "V = 9.0 - 4.0"],
        "insight": "The meter reads the difference of two internal potentials, not a drop across any one resistor. Work each branch current first, then the drop to each midpoint, and subtract. The two branches are independent dividers.",
        "shape": "circuit-reduction",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "12 - 12*2/8 - (12 - 12*6/9)", "want": "5"},
    "sol": '<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Whether you treat each branch as an independent potential divider and then take the difference of two internal potentials -- not a drop across a single resistor.</p>\n<p><b>Step 1 — the left branch current.</b> Its total resistance is <code>2.0 + 6.0 = 8.0 ohm</code>, so</p>\n<div class="formula">I_left = 12 / 8.0 = 1.5 A</div>\n<p><b>Step 2 — the potential of X.</b> X is the midpoint; below it sits the 6.0 ohm resistor, across which the drop is <code>1.5 x 6.0 = 9.0 V</code>. So X is 9.0 V above the bottom rail, equivalently <code>12 - 9.0 = 3.0 V</code> below the top -- but the relevant number is <code>V_X = 9.0 V</code> above the rail.</p>\n<p><b>Step 3 — the right branch current.</b> Its total resistance is <code>6.0 + 3.0 = 9.0 ohm</code>:</p>\n<div class="formula">I_right = 12 / 9.0 = 4/3 A</div>\n<p><b>Step 4 — the potential of Y.</b> Below Y sits the 3.0 ohm resistor, drop <code>(4/3) x 3.0 = 4.0 V</code>. So <code>V_Y = 4.0 V</code> above the bottom rail.</p>\n<p><b>Step 5 — the meter reading.</b> An ideal voltmeter draws no current, so it simply reads the potential difference between X and Y:</p>\n<div class="formula">V = V_X - V_Y = 9.0 - 4.0 = 5.0 V</div>\n<p>So <b>Answer: E.</b></p>\n<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>\n<p>&middot; <b>3.0 V</b> is the drop across the 2.0 ohm resistor on the left branch -- the distance from X up to the top rail, not the meter reading.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>8.0 V</b> is the drop across the 6.0 ohm resistor on the right branch.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>9.0 V</b> is the potential of X above the rail; a candidate who forgets to subtract Y reads the supply-split instead of the difference.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>1.5 V</b> is merely the left branch current, written as though it were a voltage.</p>\n<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> potential dividers; branch currents; an ideal voltmeter reads a potential difference.</p>',
    "trap": "Reading the drop across a single resistor (the 2.0 ohm or the 6.0 ohm) instead of the potential difference X minus Y. The meter bridges two internal points, so both midpoints must be found first and then subtracted.",
},

