# 10 — H, circuits.  Two cells in opposition, with internal resistance.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 10, "id": "S05-10", "module": "H", "diff": 3,
    "topic": "Two opposing cells: the current in the single loop",
    "rel": [("H", "Cells in opposition subtract; only the net emf drives the loop"),
            ("H", "Internal resistance adds to the external resistance in the total"),
            ("A", "Kirchhoff's loop law as a single net emf over the total resistance")],
    "key": ["cellsopposed", "internalresistance", "loopcurrent"],
    "stem": '<p>A single loop contains a 3.0 ohm resistor, a 12 V cell of internal resistance 1.0 ohm with its positive terminal uppermost, and a 6.0 V cell of internal resistance 2.0 ohm with its positive terminal lowermost, so the two cells oppose each other. {{FIG:s05-20}}</p><p>What is the current in the circuit?</p>',
    "opts": ['2.0 A', '1.0 A', '3.0 A', '0.50 A', '6.0 A'],
    "ans": 1,
    "distractors": ['drives both emfs the same way and divides by the total resistance, adding instead of subtracting', 'correct', 'adds the two emfs and the three resistances, ignoring that the cells oppose', 'divides the net emf by only the external 3.0 ohm resistor, forgetting the internal resistances', 'uses the net emf of 6 V as if it were the current in amperes'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "the cells oppose, so the net emf is 12 - 6 = 6 V (the larger cell wins)"),
            ("relate", "the total resistance is the external 3.0 ohm plus both internal resistances: 1.0 + 2.0"),
            ("eliminate", "apply the loop law as a single net emf over the total resistance"),
            ("solve", "I = 6 / (3.0 + 1.0 + 2.0) = 6 / 6.0 = 1.0 A"),
            ("check", "with the larger cell driving, the current is small and positive in the direction of the 12 V cell, which is consistent"),
        ],
        "relations": ["E_net = 12 - 6", "R_total = 3.0 + 1.0 + 2.0", "I = 6/6.0"],
        "insight": "Opposing cells subtract, and internal resistances always add to the load. The whole loop collapses to one net emf over one total resistance. Adding the emfs (because both are 'batteries') or ignoring the internal resistances are the two ways to go wrong.",
        "shape": "conservation",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "(12-6)/(3.0+1.0+2.0)", "want": "1.0"},
    "sol": '<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Two cells facing opposite ways subtract, and internal resistances add to the external resistor. The loop is then one net emf over one total resistance.</p>\n<p><b>Step 1 — the net emf.</b> The 12 V cell has its positive terminal up and the 6 V cell has its positive terminal down, so around the loop they drive in opposite senses. The 12 V cell is larger, so:</p>\n<div class="formula">E_net = 12 - 6 = 6 V</div>\n<p><b>Step 2 — the total resistance.</b> The resistor and both internal resistances are in series:</p>\n<div class="formula">R_total = 3.0 + 1.0 + 2.0 = 6.0 ohm</div>\n<p><b>Step 3 — the current.</b></p>\n<div class="formula">I = E_net / R_total = 6 / 6.0 = 1.0 A</div><p><b>Step 4 — the voltage shares.</b> The 6.0 ohm total means each ohm carries the 1.0 A, so the two internal resistances (1.0 + 2.0 = 3.0 ohm) drop <code>3.0 x 1.0 = 3.0 V</code>, leaving <code>6.0 - 3.0 = 3.0 V</code> across the external 3.0 ohm resistor -- consistent with <code>3.0 x 1.0 = 3.0 V</code>.</p><div class="formula">V_internal = (1.0 + 2.0) x 1.0 = 3.0 V ; V_load = 3.0 x 1.0 = 3.0 V</div><p>So <b>Answer: B.</b></p><p><b>Step 5 — check the sense.</b> The net emf is positive in the direction of the 12 V cell, so the current circulates that way, and its modest size (1.0 A) reflects that the two cells are largely cancelling each other.</p>\n<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>\n<p>&middot; <b>2.0 A</b> drives both emfs the same way: <code>(12 + 6)/9 = 2.0</code> -- adding when they oppose.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>3.0 A</b> adds the emfs and uses all three resistances but with the wrong combination <code>18/6</code>.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>0.50 A</b> divides the net 6 V by only the external 3.0 ohm, forgetting the internal resistances.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>6.0 A</b> mistakes the net emf of 6 V for the current in amperes.</p>\n<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> cells in series opposing; internal resistance; Kirchhoff\'s loop law.</p>',
    "trap": "Adding the two emfs because both are batteries, instead of subtracting them, or leaving out the internal resistances. The net drive is 12 - 6 = 6 V over 6.0 ohm total.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 11 — K, nuclear.  Half-life: fraction remaining after several half-lives.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 11, "id": "S05-11", "module": "K", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "Radioactive decay: the fraction remaining after a number of half-lives",
    "rel": [("K", "After each half-life the number of nuclei is halved"),
            ("K", "The fraction remaining is (1/2)^n after n half-lives"),
            ("A", "Counting the number of half-lives as total time over the half-life")],
    "key": ["halflife", "decay", "fraction"],
    "stem": '<p>A radioactive sample has a half-life of 8.0 days. What fraction of the original nuclei remains after 24 days?</p>',
    "opts": ['1/4', '1/8', '1/16', '3/8', '1/2'],
    "ans": 1,
    "distractors": ['counts only one half-life, 24/8 taken as a single halving', 'correct', 'counts four half-lives instead of three and over-halves', 'subtracts the three half-lives from one as if decay were linear', 'takes the fraction for a single half-life, the starting point not the result'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "every 8.0 days the activity and the number of nuclei halve"),
            ("relate", "24 days contains 24 / 8.0 = 3 half-lives"),
            ("eliminate", "three halvings multiply the remaining fraction by 1/2 three times"),
            ("solve", "fraction = (1/2)^3 = 1/8"),
            ("check", "the fraction is smaller than 1/2 and larger than 1/16, which brackets three halvings sensibly"),
        ],
        "relations": ["n = 24 / 8.0", "fraction = (1/2)^n", "fraction = 1/8"],
        "insight": "Decay is geometric, not linear. Three half-lives mean multiplying by 1/2 three times, giving 1/8. Any answer that adds or subtracts the half-lives is treating a halving as a subtraction.",
        "shape": "proportionality",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": False,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "(1/2)**(24/8.0)", "want": "1/8"},
    "sol": '<p><b>What is being tested.</b> The geometric nature of radioactive decay: after each half-life the remaining amount is halved, so after n half-lives it is <code>(1/2)^n</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Step 1 — how many half-lives?</b></p>\n<div class="formula">n = 24 days / 8.0 days = 3</div>\n<p><b>Step 2 — apply the halving three times.</b></p>\n<div class="formula">fraction = (1/2)^3 = 1/8</div><p><b>Step 3 — follow the decay one step at a time.</b> Rather than jump to the cube, watch each halving: after the first half-life the fraction is 1/2, after the second it is (1/2) x (1/2) = 1/4, after the third it is (1/4) x (1/2) = 1/8.</p><div class="formula">1/2 -&gt; 1/4 -&gt; 1/8 over three halvings</div><p><b>Step 4 — confirm the time divides exactly.</b> 24 days is three exact 8.0-day intervals with no remainder, so there is no partial fourth half-life to account for; the fraction is the clean value (1/2)^3.</p><p>So <b>Answer: B.</b></p><p><b>Step 5 — check the bracket.</b> Three halvings must give something below 1/2 (one halving) but above 1/16 (four halvings). 1/8 sits exactly there.</p>\n<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>\n<p>&middot; <b>1/4</b> is the result of only two half-lives, as if 24/8 had been read as two.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>1/16</b> is four half-lives -- one too many.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>3/8</b> subtracts the three half-lives from 1, treating decay as a linear loss.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>1/2</b> is the fraction after a single half-life, the start of the process rather than its end.</p>\n<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> half-life; geometric decay; the (1/2)^n rule.</p>',
    "trap": "Treating the three half-lives as a subtraction from 1 (giving 3/8) instead of three multiplications by 1/2. Decay is geometric, so each half-life multiplies the remainder by 1/2.",
},

