# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 14 — B, kinematics.  Horizontal projectile: time of fall and range.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 14, "id": "S05-14", "module": "B", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "A horizontal projectile: the range from the time of fall",
    "rel": [("B", "Vertical and horizontal motion are independent; the fall time depends only on height"),
            ("B", "Constant horizontal velocity gives range = horizontal speed x time of fall"),
            ("A", "Using t = sqrt(2h/g) and then range = v t")],
    "key": ["projectile", "range", "timeoffall"],
    "stem": '<p>A ball is thrown horizontally at 12 m/s from a height of 20 m above level ground. (Use g = 10 m/s^2.) How far from the point directly below the launch does it land?</p>',
    "opts": ['12 m', '48 m', '24 m', '34 m', '17 m'],
    "ans": 2,
    "distractors": ['uses the launch speed as the time of fall, confusing a speed with a time', 'doubles the correct range, perhaps using 2t or adding the height', 'correct', 'a muddled combination that lands between the right answer and a doubled one', 'uses sqrt(2h/g) as the range without multiplying by the horizontal speed'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "the vertical motion is free fall from 20 m; horizontally the ball keeps its 12 m/s"),
            ("relate", "time to fall: h = 1/2 g t^2, so t = sqrt(2h/g)"),
            ("eliminate", "the range is the horizontal speed times that time, because the two motions are independent"),
            ("solve", "t = sqrt(2 x 20 / 10) = 2.0 s; range = 12 x 2.0 = 24 m"),
            ("check", "the range is larger than the height and of the order of v times a couple of seconds, which is sane"),
        ],
        "relations": ["h = 1/2 g t^2", "t = sqrt(2h/g)", "range = v t"],
        "insight": "A horizontal launch splits cleanly: the time of fall is fixed by the height alone (vertical motion), and the range is just horizontal speed times that time. Mixing the two -- using the speed as a time, or doubling -- produces every distractor.",
        "shape": "superposition",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": False,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "12*sqrt(2*20/10)", "want": "24"},
    "sol": '<p><b>What is being tested.</b> The independence of horizontal and vertical motion for a projectile launched horizontally: the fall time comes from the height, the range from the horizontal speed.</p>\n<p><b>Step 1 — the time of fall.</b> Vertically the ball starts with no speed and drops 20 m under gravity:</p>\n<div class="formula">h = 1/2 g t^2   =>   t = sqrt(2h/g) = sqrt(2 x 20 / 10) = sqrt(4) = 2.0 s</div>\n<p><b>Step 2 — the range.</b> Horizontally the speed stays 12 m/s, so in 2.0 s the ball travels</p>\n<div class="formula">range = 12 x 2.0 = 24 m</div><p><b>Step 3 — why the fall time also sets the horizontal distance.</b> Horizontal and vertical motion are independent: the 12 m/s launch does nothing to how fast the ball falls, so the 2.0 s it takes to drop is the same 2.0 s available for horizontal travel. That is what licenses the relation range = speed x time.</p><div class="formula">horizontal distance = v_x x t_fall = 12 x 2.0</div><p><b>Step 4 — check the units and the scale.</b> sqrt(2h/g) has units sqrt(m / (m/s^2)) = sqrt(s^2) = s, so the time is dimensionally sound; and a 20 m drop yielding a 2 s fall, stretched by 12 m/s, lands at 24 m -- the right order.</p><p>So <b>Answer: C.</b></p><p><b>Step 5 — check the answer.</b> The range should exceed the 20 m height (it does) and be of order the speed times a couple of seconds. 24 m fits.</p>\n<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>\n<p>&middot; <b>12 m</b> uses the 12 m/s launch speed as if it were the time of fall.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>48 m</b> doubles the correct range, perhaps using <code>2 t</code> or adding the height.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>34 m</b> is a muddled blend that sits between 24 and 48.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>17 m</b> takes <code>sqrt(2h/g)</code> as the range and never multiplies by the horizontal speed.</p>\n<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> horizontal projectile; independent components; range = v_x t.</p>',
    "trap": "Using the 12 m/s launch speed as the time of fall, or doubling the result. The time of fall is sqrt(2h/g) = 2.0 s, and the range is horizontal speed (12 m/s) times that time.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 15 — C, forces.  Equilibrium under two perpendicular forces.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 15, "id": "S05-15", "module": "C", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "Two perpendicular forces: the equilibrating force",
    "rel": [("C", "The resultant of two perpendicular forces is found by Pythagoras"),
            ("C", "A third force in equilibrium is equal and opposite to that resultant"),
            ("A", "Forming the vector sum and then its negative")],
    "key": ["resultant", "equilibrium", "pythagoras"],
    "stem": '<p>Two forces, 3 N and 4 N, act at right angles on a body. What single force is needed to keep the body in equilibrium?</p>',
    "opts": ['7 N', '1 N', '12 N', '3.5 N', '5 N'],
    "ans": 4,
    "distractors": ['adds the two forces as if they were parallel, not perpendicular', 'subtracts them as if they acted along a line, giving the difference', 'multiplies the two magnitudes, a dimensionally wrong combination', 'averages the two forces instead of combining them as vectors', 'correct'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "the resultant R of the 3 N and 4 N forces at right angles satisfies R^2 = 3^2 + 4^2"),
            ("relate", "R = 5 N, directed between the two given forces"),
            ("eliminate", "for equilibrium the third force must be equal and opposite to R"),
            ("solve", "the required force has magnitude 5 N, opposite to the resultant"),
            ("check", "it is larger than either given force but smaller than their sum, as a vector sum should be"),
        ],
        "relations": ["R^2 = 3^2 + 4^2", "R = 5 N", "equilibrant = 5 N opposite"],
        "insight": "Perpendicular forces combine by Pythagoras: 3-4-5. The equilibrium force is the negative of that resultant, so it has the same magnitude, 5 N. Adding them as parallel (7 N) or subtracting (1 N) ignores the right angle, which is the trap.",
        "shape": "algebraic-elimination",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": False,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "sqrt(3**2+4**2)", "want": "5"},
    "sol": '<p><b>What is being tested.</b> Combining two perpendicular forces by Pythagoras, then realising the equilibrium force is equal and opposite to their resultant.</p>\n<p><b>Step 1 — the resultant of the two given forces.</b> They are at right angles, so the resultant R has</p>\n<div class="formula">R^2 = 3^2 + 4^2 = 9 + 16 = 25\nR = 5 N</div>\n<p><b>Step 2 — the equilibrium force.</b> For the body to be in equilibrium the vector sum of all forces must be zero, so the third force must cancel R: same magnitude, opposite direction.</p>\n<div class="formula">required force = 5 N, opposite to the resultant</div><p><b>Step 3 — resolve the two forces onto axes.</b> Put the 3 N force along x and the 4 N force along y. Their resultant then has components R_x = 3 N and R_y = 4 N -- a vector sum, not an arithmetic one.</p><p><b>Step 4 — recover the magnitude from the components.</b> Because the components are perpendicular, Pythagoras gives the magnitude:</p><div class="formula">R = sqrt(R_x^2 + R_y^2) = sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = 5 N</div><p>So <b>Answer: E.</b></p><p><b>Step 5 — check the size.</b> A vector sum of 3 N and 4 N must exceed each but fall short of their sum 7 N. 5 N does exactly that.</p>\n<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>\n<p>&middot; <b>7 N</b> adds the forces as if parallel (3 + 4), ignoring the right angle.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>1 N</b> subtracts them (4 - 3), as if they opposed along one line.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>12 N</b> multiplies them, which is not even a force dimensionally.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>3.5 N</b> averages them, another way to ignore the geometry.</p>\n<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> resultant of perpendicular forces; Pythagoras; equilibrium as zero vector sum.</p>',
    "trap": "Adding the two forces as parallel (7 N) or subtracting them (1 N) instead of combining them as vectors at right angles. The resultant is sqrt(3^2 + 4^2) = 5 N, and the equilibrant is 5 N opposite.",
},

