{
    "n": 16, "id": "S05-16", "module": "D", "diff": 3,
    "topic": "A rotating sphere: how latitude changes the centripetal acceleration",
    "rel": [("D", "A point at latitude lambda moves in a circle of radius r cos lambda about the spin axis"),
            ("D", "Centripetal acceleration is omega^2 times that circular radius"),
            ("A", "Forming the ratio of the acceleration at latitude to that at the equator")],
    "key": ["latitude", "centripetal", "rotationaxis"],
    "stem": '<p>A sphere of radius R rotates with angular speed omega about a vertical axis. A point at latitude lambda moves in a circle about that axis. {{FIG:s05-16}}</p><p>By what factor is the centripetal acceleration of that point less than the centripetal acceleration of a point on the equator?</p>',
    "opts": ['sin lambda', 'cos^2 lambda', 'sin^2 lambda', '1', 'cos lambda'],
    "ans": 4,
    "distractors": ['uses sin lambda, the wrong trigonometric factor for the distance from the axis', 'squares the cosine, double-counting the latitude dependence', 'squares the sine, the wrong factor squared', 'claims the acceleration is the same everywhere, ignoring latitude', 'correct'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "the point at latitude lambda is a distance R cos lambda from the rotation axis, not R"),
            ("relate", "its centripetal acceleration is omega^2 (R cos lambda), whereas the equator has omega^2 R"),
            ("eliminate", "the ratio is therefore (R cos lambda) / R = cos lambda"),
            ("solve", "at lambda = 45 degrees the factor is cos 45 = sqrt(2)/2, about 0.71"),
            ("check", "the factor is 1 at the equator and falls to 0 at the poles, which cosine does and the others do not"),
        ],
        "relations": ["r_circle = R cos lambda", "a = omega^2 r_circle", "ratio = cos lambda"],
        "insight": "Only the distance from the spin axis matters for centripetal acceleration, and that distance is R cos(lambda), not R. So the acceleration falls as cos(lambda): 1 at the equator, 0 at the poles. Using sin, or squaring, or calling it constant, each misses that one geometry fact.",
        "shape": "ratio-cancellation",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": True,
        "figure_essential": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "cos(45*pi/180)", "want": "sqrt(2)/2"},
    "sol": '<p><b>What is being tested.</b> That centripetal acceleration depends on the distance from the rotation axis, which for a point at latitude lambda is <code>R cos(lambda)</code>, not R.</p>\n<p><b>Step 1 — the radius of the circular path.</b> The sphere spins about its vertical axis. A point at latitude lambda is on a circle whose radius is its distance from that axis:</p>\n<div class="formula">r_circle = R cos lambda</div>\n<p>(At lambda = 0, the equator, this is R; at the poles it is 0.)</p>\n<p><b>Step 2 — the centripetal acceleration.</b></p>\n<div class="formula">a = omega^2 r_circle = omega^2 R cos lambda</div>\n<p>On the equator the acceleration is <code>omega^2 R</code>. The ratio is therefore</p>\n<div class="formula">ratio = (omega^2 R cos lambda) / (omega^2 R) = cos lambda</div><p><b>Step 3 — derive the latitude radius from the geometry.</b> At latitude lambda the point sits at height <code>R sin lambda</code> above the equatorial plane, so by Pythagoras its horizontal distance from the axis is <code>sqrt(R^2 - (R sin lambda)^2) = R cos lambda</code>. That is why the equator (lambda = 0) gives R and the poles give 0 -- the geometry, not a guess.</p><div class="formula">r_circle = sqrt(R^2 - R^2 sin^2 lambda) = R cos lambda</div><p><b>Step 4 — apply the centripetal formula to that radius.</b> Since <code>a = omega^2 r_circle</code>, substituting the latitude radius gives <code>a = omega^2 R cos lambda</code>; dividing by the equatorial value <code>omega^2 R</code> leaves <code>cos lambda</code>, with no extra factor.</p><p>So <b>Answer: E.</b></p><p><b>Step 5 — check the endpoints.</b> At the equator (lambda = 0) the factor is <code>cos 0 = 1</code>; at the poles (lambda = 90) it is <code>cos 90 = 0</code>. Cosine is the only option that does both, and at 45 degrees it gives <code>sqrt(2)/2</code> (about 0.71).</p>\n<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>\n<p>&middot; <b>sin lambda</b> uses the wrong trigonometric factor for the distance from the axis.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>cos^2 lambda</b> squares the correct factor, double-counting the latitude dependence.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>sin^2 lambda</b> is the wrong factor squared.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>1</b> claims the acceleration is the same everywhere, ignoring latitude entirely.</p>\n<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> circular motion on a rotating sphere; the radius of the latitude circle; centripetal acceleration and latitude.</p>',
    "trap": "Using R instead of R cos(lambda) for the radius of the latitude circle, or sin(lambda), or squaring. The distance from the spin axis is R cos(lambda), so the acceleration scales as cos(lambda): 1 at the equator, 0 at the poles.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 17 — E, materials.  A square lamina with a corner removed: the centre of mass.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 17, "id": "S05-17", "module": "E", "diff": 3,
    "topic": "A square lamina with a corner removed: the shift of the centre of mass",
    "rel": [("E", "The missing corner can be treated as negative mass for finding the new centre of mass"),
            ("E", "The centre of mass is (sum of mass x position) / total mass for each axis"),
            ("A", "Working with area as mass since the lamina is uniform")],
    "key": ["com", "negmass", "lamina"],
    "stem": '<p>A uniform square lamina has side 2a. A square of side a is removed from its upper-left corner. {{FIG:s05-15}}</p><p>By what distance is the centre of mass displaced horizontally from the centre of the original uncut square?</p>',
    "opts": ['a/3', 'a/6', 'a/4', 'a/2', 'a/12'],
    "ans": 1,
    "distractors": ['uses a/3, as if the removed corner were a third of the mass rather than a quarter', 'correct', 'uses a/4, the size of the removed side, confused with the displacement', 'uses a/2, the half-side of the lamina, not a displacement at all', 'uses a/12, a quarter of a/3, from double-reducing the moment'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "take the original square as mass 4 units (area 4a^2) centred at (a, a); the removed corner is mass 1 unit at (a/2, 3a/2)"),
            ("relate", "the remaining lamina has mass 3 units; its centre of mass is the original minus the corner, weighted by mass"),
            ("eliminate", "x_new = (4a - 1 x a/2) / 3 = (7a/2) / 3 = 7a/6, so the displacement from x = a is 7a/6 - a = a/6"),
            ("solve", "the horizontal shift is a/6 to the right"),
            ("check", "the shift is small and toward the remaining mass, which is to the right and below the cut, as expected"),
        ],
        "relations": ["M_orig = 4, M_cut = 1", "x_new = (4a - a/2)/3", "shift = 7a/6 - a = a/6"],
        "insight": "Treat the missing corner as negative mass. The new centre of mass is (original moment minus corner moment) divided by the remaining mass. The displacement comes out a/6 -- small, and toward the bulk of the remaining L-shape, which is the physical check.",
        "shape": "diagram-geometry",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": True,
        "figure_essential": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "7*a/6 - a", "want": "a/6"},
    "sol": '<p><b>What is being tested.</b> The negative-mass method for a centre of mass: the cut-out is subtracted from the whole, and the remaining lamina is the difference, weighted by area (which stands in for mass for a uniform sheet).</p>\n<p><b>Step 1 — set up coordinates.</b> Let the original square run from <code>(0,0)</code> to <code>(2a, 2a)</code>, so its centre is at <code>(a, a)</code> and its area is <code>4a^2</code>. The removed upper-left corner runs from <code>(0, a)</code> to <code>(a, 2a)</code>, area <code>a^2</code>, centre at <code>(a/2, 3a/2)</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2 — the remaining lamina.</b> Its area is <code>4a^2 - a^2 = 3a^2</code>. By the negative-mass idea:</p>\n<div class="formula">x_new = (4a^2 x a - a^2 x a/2) / (3a^2)</div>\n<p><b>Step 3 — the horizontal position.</b> Cancel <code>a^2</code>:</p>\n<div class="formula">x_new = (4a - a/2) / 3 = (7a/2) / 3 = 7a/6</div>\n<p><b>Step 4 — the displacement.</b> The original centre was at <code>x = a</code>, so the shift is</p>\n<div class="formula">shift = 7a/6 - a = a/6</div>\n<p>to the right, toward the remaining material. So <b>Answer: B.</b></p>\n<p><b>Step 5 — check.</b> The cut is a quarter of the area at the upper left, so the centre of mass should move a little to the right and down -- a/6 in x is small and in the right direction. (The vertical shift is a/6 down as well, by symmetry of the cut.)</p>\n<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>\n<p>&middot; <b>a/3</b> treats the removed corner as a third of the mass rather than a quarter.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>a/4</b> confuses the side of the cut-out with the displacement.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>a/2</b> is the half-side of the whole lamina, not a displacement at all.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>a/12</b> double-reduces the moment and lands a quarter of the a/3 error.</p>\n<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> centre of mass; the negative-mass (cut-out) method; uniform lamina as area.</p>',
    "trap": "Treating the removed corner as a third of the mass, or confusing the cut-out's side with the displacement. The corner is a quarter of the area, and the new centre of mass is (original moment minus corner moment) over the remaining three-quarter mass, giving a shift of a/6.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 18 — F, waves.  Two-source interference: the fringe spacing.
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 18, "id": "S05-18", "module": "F", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "Two coherent sources: the fringe spacing",
    "rel": [("F", "Fringe spacing beta = lambda D / d for two slits separated by d at distance D"),
            ("F", "All quantities must be in the same length unit before dividing"),
            ("A", "Converting mm and nm to metres, or keeping a consistent scale")],
    "key": ["interference", "fringespacing", "twosources"],
    "stem": '<p>Two coherent sources 1.0 mm apart illuminate a screen 1.5 m away. For light of wavelength 500 nm, what is the fringe spacing on the screen?</p>',
    "opts": ['0.25 mm', '1.5 mm', '2.5 mm', '0.75 mm', '0.50 mm'],
    "ans": 3,
    "distractors": ['uses d in the numerator and lambda D in the denominator, inverting the formula', 'treats the 1.5 m screen distance as the fringe spacing itself', 'a muddled value from mixing the units without conversion', 'correct', 'swaps lambda and d, putting the separation in the numerator with no D'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "fringe spacing beta = lambda D / d"),
            ("relate", "convert to metres: lambda = 5.0 x 10^-7 m, d = 3.0 x 10^-4 m, D = 1.5 m"),
            ("eliminate", "substitute, keeping the units consistent"),
            ("solve", "beta = (5.0 x 10^-7 x 1.5) / (1.0 x 10^-3) = 7.5 x 10^-4 m = 0.75 mm"),
            ("check", "the spacing is smaller than the wavelength times the D/d ratio suggests and is a sub-millimetre quantity, which is right for visible light"),
        ],
        "relations": ["beta = lambda D / d", "lambda = 5.0e-7 m", "d = 1.0e-3 m", "beta = 7.5e-4 m"],
        "insight": "Fringe spacing is lambda D / d. The only way to fail is a unit slip: lambda in nm, d in mm, D in m must all become metres before dividing. Invert the formula, or swap lambda and d, and you get a distractor.",
        "shape": "proportionality",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": False,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "(500e-9*1.5)/(1.0e-3)", "want": "0.00075"},
    "sol": '<p><b>What is being tested.</b> The two-source fringe spacing <code>beta = lambda D / d</code>, and the discipline of converting every length to the same unit first.</p>\n<p><b>Step 1 — the formula.</b></p>\n<div class="formula">beta = lambda D / d</div>\n<p><b>Step 2 — consistent units (metres).</b></p>\n<div class="formula">lambda = 500 nm = 5.0 x 10^-7 m\nd = 1.0 mm = 1.0 x 10^-3 m\nD = 1.5 m</div>\n<p><b>Step 3 — substitute.</b></p>\n<div class="formula">beta = (5.0 x 10^-7 x 1.5) / (3.0 x 10^-4)\n= 7.5 x 10^-4 m\n= 0.75 mm</div>\n<p>So <b>Answer: D.</b></p>\n<p><b>Step 4 — check.</b> For visible light and a millimetre-scale slit spacing the fringes are sub-millimetre, so 0.75 mm is the right order; a value of several millimetres would mean the formula or units were wrong.</p>\n<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>\n<p>&middot; <b>0.25 mm</b> inverts the formula to <code>lambda d / D</code>.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>1.5 mm</b> offers the screen distance D itself.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>2.5 mm</b> is a unit-muddled value from mixing mm and nm.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>0.50 mm</b> swaps lambda and d, putting the 0.30 mm separation in the numerator with no D.</p>\n<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> two-source interference; beta = lambda D / d; unit consistency.</p>',
    "trap": "Inverting the fringe-spacing formula (lambda d / D) or forgetting to convert nm and mm to metres. beta = lambda D / d, and with lambda = 5.0e-7 m, d = 1.0e-3 m, D = 1.5 m it is 0.75 mm.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 19 — G, optics.  Critical angle (asks for sin of it, to stay calculator-free).
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 19, "id": "S05-19", "module": "G", "diff": 2,
    "topic": "The critical angle: its sine for glass to air",
    "rel": [("G", "At the critical angle, refraction is along the boundary so sin theta_c = n2 / n1"),
            ("G", "For glass (n = 1.50) to air (n = 1.00), sin theta_c = 1/1.50"),
            ("A", "Inverting the refractive index, not the other way round")],
    "key": ["criticalangle", "totalinternal", "sine"],
    "stem": '<p>Light travels from glass of refractive index 1.50 to air (n = 1.00). What is sin of the critical angle for this interface?</p>',
    "opts": ['0.67', '1.50', '0.50', '0.33', '1.00'],
    "ans": 0,
    "distractors": ['correct', 'uses the refractive index itself, 1.50, as the value instead of its reciprocal', 'takes 0.50 as if the index were 2.0 rather than 1.50', 'uses (n1 - n2)/n1 = 1/3, a difference-of-indices slip rather than the ratio n2/n1', 'claims the sine is 1, which would mean no critical angle exists'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "at the critical angle the refracted ray runs along the boundary, so Snell gives n1 sin theta_c = n2 sin 90"),
            ("relate", "with n1 = 1.50 (glass) and n2 = 1.00 (air), sin theta_c = 1.00 / 1.50"),
            ("eliminate", "1 / 1.50 = 2/3, about 0.67"),
            ("solve", "sin theta_c = 2/3 ≈ 0.67"),
            ("check", "the sine is less than 1 and larger than 0.5, which brackets a critical angle of roughly 42 degrees"),
        ],
        "relations": ["n1 sin theta_c = n2", "sin theta_c = 1.00/1.50", "sin theta_c = 2/3"],
        "insight": "The critical angle comes from n1 sin(theta_c) = n2 with the second medium at the limit, so sin(theta_c) = n2/n1 = 2/3 -- the reciprocal of the refractive index, not the index itself (1.50). The traps are the index read directly, the half-index, the difference error, and sin = 1.",
        "shape": "monotonicity",
        "approx": False,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": False,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "1/1.5", "want": "2/3"},
    "sol": '<p><b>What is being tested.</b> The critical-angle condition and, specifically, that its sine is the reciprocal of the refractive index (<code>n2/n1</code>), not the index itself.</p>\n<p><b>Step 1 — the condition.</b> At the critical angle the refracted ray grazes the boundary, so the refracted angle is 90 degrees and Snell\'s law reads</p>\n<div class="formula">n1 sin theta_c = n2 sin 90 = n2</div>\n<p><b>Step 2 — glass to air.</b> Here <code>n1 = 1.50</code> (glass) and <code>n2 = 1.00</code> (air):</p>\n<div class="formula">sin theta_c = n2 / n1 = 1.00 / 1.50 = 2/3</div>\n<p><b>Step 3 — the value.</b> <code>2/3</code> is about 0.67. So <b>Answer: A.</b></p>\n<p><b>Step 4 — check.</b> The sine must be below 1 (a critical angle exists only because glass is denser than air) and above 0.5 (the critical angle is below 45 degrees since 1/1.5 &gt; 1/√2). 0.67 sits correctly between.</p>\n<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>\n<p>&middot; <b>1.50</b> is the refractive index read directly as the sine, instead of its reciprocal 2/3.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>0.50</b> would be the sine if the glass index were 2.00 rather than 1.50.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>0.33</b> comes from a difference error, (n1 - n2)/n1 = 1/3, instead of the ratio n2/n1.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>1.00</b> says sin(theta_c) = 1, which would mean there is no critical angle -- but glass to air does have one.</p>\n<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> total internal reflection; the critical angle; sin(theta_c) = n2/n1.</p>',
    "trap": "Giving the refractive index 1.50 directly, or 0.50 (as if the index were 2.0), or 1/3 from a difference error, or 1.00. The sine is n2/n1 = 2/3, the reciprocal of the index.",
},

# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# 20 — H, circuits.  Diode characteristic + 100 ohm load line (reading the graph).
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
{
    "n": 20, "id": "S05-20", "module": "H", "diff": 3,
    "topic": "A diode and a load resistor: the operating current from the load line",
    "rel": [("H", "The load line is V_supply = V_diode + I R_load, a straight line on the I-V plane"),
            ("H", "The operating point is where the load line meets the diode's characteristic"),
            ("A", "Reading the characteristic's straight segment and solving the intersection")],
    "key": ["loadline", "diode", "operatingpoint"],
    "stem": '<p>A diode whose characteristic is shown is connected in series with a 100 ohm resistor and a 1.0 V supply. {{FIG:s05-10}}</p><p>What current flows? (On the drawn characteristic the current rises linearly from 0.55 V and passes through 22 mA at 0.8 V.)</p>',
    "opts": ['10 mA', '22 mA', '4.0 mA', '2.0 mA', '6.0 mA'],
    "ans": 2,
    "distractors": ['reads the short-circuit current, the load-line intercept on the current axis, as the answer', 'reads the 22 mA point straight off the curve, ignoring that the resistor limits the current', 'correct', 'reads the current at 0.8 V on the load line, not the intersection', 'a value between the two intercepts that is not the true intersection'],
    "profile": {
        "steps": [
            ("relate", "the load line has intercepts V = 1.0 V (I = 0) and I = 1.0/100 = 10 mA (V = 0)"),
            ("relate", "on the diode's straight segment, I(mA) = 22 (V - 0.55) / (0.82 - 0.55) for 0.55 < V < 0.82"),
            ("eliminate", "the operating point satisfies both: V = 1.0 - 0.1 I_mA and the diode line"),
            ("solve", "I_mA = 22 (0.45 - 0.1 I_mA) / 0.27, giving I_mA = 9.9 / (0.27 + 2.2) ≈ 4.0 mA"),
            ("check", "the current is well below the 10 mA intercept and the diode voltage is about 0.6 V, on the steep part of the curve, as the drawing shows"),
        ],
        "relations": ["V = 1.0 - 0.1 I_mA", "I_mA = 22 (V - 0.55)/0.27", "I ≈ 4.0 mA"],
        "insight": "The load line is a straight constraint from the resistor and battery; the diode curve is the device. They meet at the operating point, and you solve the intersection -- not the short-circuit current, not a point read off one curve alone. The intercepts and the curve's slope together pin it at about 4 mA.",
        "shape": "conservation",
        "approx": True,
        "symbolic": False,
        "figure_essential": True,
    },
    "check": {"kind": "eval", "expr": "22*0.45/(0.27+22*0.1)", "want": "990/247"},
    "sol": '<p><b>What is being tested.</b> The load-line construction: a resistor and battery impose a straight line on the diode\'s I-V plane, and the operating current is where that line meets the device curve -- not a point read off either alone.</p>\n<p><b>Step 1 — the load line.</b> The supply is 1.0 V and the resistor is 100 ohm. In mA, a voltage V across the diode leaves <code>1.0 - V</code> across the resistor, carrying current <code>(1.0 - V)/100</code> amps = <code>10(1.0 - V)</code> mA. So</p>\n<div class="formula">I_mA = 10 (1.0 - V)   =>   V = 1.0 - 0.1 I_mA</div>\n<p>This line runs from <code>(V=1.0, I=0)</code> to <code>(V=0, I=10 mA)</code>.</p>\n<p><b>Step 2 — the diode segment.</b> Between 0.55 V and 0.82 V the characteristic is straight and passes through 22 mA at 0.8 V. Its slope is</p>\n<div class="formula">I_mA = 22 (V - 0.55) / (0.82 - 0.55) = 22 (V - 0.55) / 0.27</div>\n<p><b>Step 3 — intersect.</b> Substitute <code>V = 1.0 - 0.1 I_mA</code> into the diode line:</p>\n<div class="formula">I_mA = 22 (1.0 - 0.1 I_mA - 0.55) / 0.27\n= 22 (0.45 - 0.1 I_mA) / 0.27</div>\n<p>Multiply out: <code>0.27 I_mA = 9.9 - 2.2 I_mA</code>, so <code>(0.27 + 2.2) I_mA = 9.9</code>, and</p>\n<div class="formula">I_mA = 9.9 / (0.27 + 2.2) ≈ 4.0 mA</div>\n<p>So <b>Answer: C.</b></p>\n<p><b>Step 4 — check.</b> At 4.0 mA the resistor drops <code>0.1 x 4.0 = 0.4 V</code>, so the diode has about 0.6 V across it -- on the steep part of the curve, consistent with the drawing. The current is well below the 10 mA intercept, as it must be.</p>\n<p><b>The distractors.</b></p>\n<p>&middot; <b>10 mA</b> is the short-circuit intercept of the load line, where the diode voltage is forced to zero.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>22 mA</b> is the point on the diode curve at 0.8 V, read with no regard for the resistor.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>2.0 mA</b> is the current the load line would allow at 0.8 V across the diode (<code>(1.0-0.8)/100 = 2 mA</code>), not the intersection.</p>\n<p>&middot; <b>6.0 mA</b> is a value between the two intercepts that is not the true meeting point.</p>\n<p><b>Relevant topics:</b> load-line analysis; the diode I-V characteristic; solving the operating point.</p>',
    "trap": "Reading the 22 mA point off the diode curve, or the 10 mA short-circuit intercept, as the answer. The current is set by the intersection of the load line V = 1.0 - 0.1 I_mA with the diode segment, which is about 4 mA.",
},

